WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.920
DITCHING CHINA:
In early 2010,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:01.920 --> 00:00:07.860
Google proudly declared that they were done with&nbsp;
China. They weren’t gonna put up with censorship,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.860 --> 00:00:14.400
propaganda, or the CCP. Google was built to make&nbsp;
the all information easily accessible and useful,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:20.160
not just the information that the CCP liked.&nbsp;
With that, Google began redirecting all search&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.160 --> 00:00:25.440
traffic from China to an uncensored Hong&nbsp;
Kong version of Google. For obvious reasons,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:32.400
the CCP was livid. This was their country and&nbsp;
they got to set the rules. The only reason&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:38.220
they even let Google in was because Google had&nbsp;
agreed to play by the rules years ago. But now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.220 --> 00:00:44.940
they suddenly wanted to fight censorship? Hell no,&nbsp;
that was unacceptable. The CCP would threaten to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.940 --> 00:00:49.920
ban Google entirely if they didn’t return to their&nbsp;
previous arrangement. Google’s CEO at the time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:55.620
Eric Schmidt, was more than happy to do this. He&nbsp;
had worked years to build up relations with China,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.620 --> 00:01:01.080
and he didn’t want to lose out the world’s&nbsp;
largest consumer market just to fight this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.080 --> 00:01:06.600
moral battle. After all, China wasn’t gonna&nbsp;
change their policies on censorship just because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:11.640
Google left the country. They would find someone&nbsp;
else who’s willing to play ball by their rules,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.640 --> 00:01:18.120
so Google might as well be that person. Google’s&nbsp;
founders, Larry Page and Sergey Brin however, had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.120 --> 00:01:24.300
a different perspective. They felt that principles&nbsp;
should always come before profit and here, it was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.300 --> 00:01:30.300
clear that China was asking Google to bend their&nbsp;
principles, and this duo was no longer willing to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.300 --> 00:01:31.330
do that. They also weren’t intimated by China’s&nbsp;
threats to ban them. Who were they to ban Google?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.330 --> 00:01:33.660
Google was the one that was gonna ban China.&nbsp;
They proudly announced “We believe strongly that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.660 --> 00:01:39.000
in the long term, we will be better served — as&nbsp;
shareholders and in all other ways — by a company&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:45.720
that does good things for the world." And with&nbsp;
that, Google had left China. Starting in March 31,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.720 --> 00:01:52.440
2010, all Google searches from mainland China were&nbsp;
met with a DNS error. Google had made their stance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.440 --> 00:01:56.940
on censorship crystal clear and they would simply&nbsp;
double down on this stance in the coming years.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.940 --> 00:02:02.640
I don’t think you’d be surprised to hear that&nbsp;
their marketshare in China would get crushed as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.640 --> 00:02:09.660
a result. In 2010, Google boasted a solid 29%&nbsp;
marketshare in China, but by the end of 2013,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.660 --> 00:02:16.680
this number would drop to just 1.7%. Google had&nbsp;
effectively forfeited their chances in China,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.680 --> 00:02:22.560
so Larry and Sergey figured that they might&nbsp;
as well make it official. Starting in 2014,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.560 --> 00:02:30.180
Google, Gmail, Chrome, YouTube, and more would all&nbsp;
become unavailable in mainland China. This was the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.180 --> 00:02:37.020
legacy of Larry Page and Sergey Brin in China, but&nbsp;
all of this would change in 2015. In late 2015,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.020 --> 00:02:43.620
a new CEO would be announced: Sundar Pichai. From&nbsp;
very early on, Sundar would begin defending the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.620 --> 00:02:48.060
idea of Google returning to China. He would&nbsp;
go onto host a developer conference in China,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.060 --> 00:02:53.100
and launch a bunch of developer tools.&nbsp;
These included developers.google.cn,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.100 --> 00:02:59.580
developer.android.cn, and firebase.google.cn.&nbsp;
Since then, Google has been hard at work&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.580 --> 00:03:05.100
rebuilding their relationship with China. In&nbsp;
fact, it’s alleged that at one point they were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.100 --> 00:03:10.260
even building a spying tool for China. So,&nbsp;
here’s how Google went from ditching China to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.260 --> 00:03:15.780
fight censorship to building a surveillance tool&nbsp;
just to get back in the good graces of the CCP.

00:03:15.780 --> 00:03:18.720
A TRUCE:&nbsp;

00:03:18.720 --> 00:03:27.840
Taking a look back, Google has been flirting with&nbsp;
the Chinese market for well over two decades now.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.840 --> 00:03:32.580
Their earliest attempt to appeal to a Chinese&nbsp;
audience can be traced all the way back to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.580 --> 00:03:38.100
September of 2000. This is when Google announced&nbsp;
the ability to use Google.com in simplified and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.100 --> 00:03:43.440
traditional Chinese. But, it wasn’t until&nbsp;
Eric Schmidt took over as CEO in 2001 that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.440 --> 00:03:49.560
Google really started pursuing the Chinese market.&nbsp;
China was never a top priority though. After all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.560 --> 00:03:53.940
Google was still working on launching a&nbsp;
bunch of foundational products. Keep in mind,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.940 --> 00:03:59.520
Gmail and YouTube weren't even a thing yet.&nbsp;
But, as Google’s western product stack matured&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.520 --> 00:04:04.800
and the company went public, Eric turned his&nbsp;
focus onto the Chinese market. To him, it was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.800 --> 00:04:10.440
clear that this was the next frontier along with&nbsp;
India. But, given that India was an open market,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.440 --> 00:04:15.480
he would spend much of international effort&nbsp;
on cracking China. Fortunately for Google,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.480 --> 00:04:20.580
China was surprisingly open to letting Google&nbsp;
operate in the country as long as they stuck&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.580 --> 00:04:26.400
to some ground rules, primarily don’t show stuff&nbsp;
that makes us look bad. From the very beginning,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.400 --> 00:04:31.740
this became a point of controversy for&nbsp;
anti-censorship advocates around the world. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.740 --> 00:04:37.260
Larry and Sergey had a much more optimistic&nbsp;
perspective. They figured, sure we’ll have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.260 --> 00:04:42.780
to censor if we enter China, but we can probably&nbsp;
get away with less censorship than native Chinese&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.780 --> 00:04:49.140
search engines. So, they were still effectively&nbsp;
reducing censorship in China overall. And with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.140 --> 00:04:55.740
that in mind, Google.cn would launch in 2006. From&nbsp;
the very beginning, Google would begin pushing the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.740 --> 00:05:01.560
boundaries with China. Not only did they censor&nbsp;
less results, but when they did censor results,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.560 --> 00:05:07.740
they had a pop up telling users that results&nbsp;
had been censored. This move pissed of the CCP&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.740 --> 00:05:13.260
as Google didn’t ask for permission before&nbsp;
implementing this pop up. The CCP said that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.260 --> 00:05:18.840
this move was like coming over for dinner and&nbsp;
saying ‘I’ll eat the food, but I don’t like it’.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.840 --> 00:05:23.100
To make things worse, Google’s rebellious&nbsp;
characteristics tended to rub off on Chinese&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.100 --> 00:05:28.560
search engines like Baidu. Really, Chinese&nbsp;
search engines had no choice but to follow suit.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.560 --> 00:05:34.980
Otherwise, they would simply bleed marketshare&nbsp;
to Google. While the CCP was no doubt unhappy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.980 --> 00:05:40.680
they also weren’t willing to push out Google&nbsp;
so early. You see, much of the Chinese internet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.680 --> 00:05:46.200
was just bad knockoffs of American websites.&nbsp;
Google was one of the first well engineered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.200 --> 00:05:50.400
platforms to be launched in the country, so&nbsp;
it was in their best interest to keep Google&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.400 --> 00:05:54.874
around as they brought in a substantial amount&nbsp;
of knowledge, funding, and talent. The bottomline&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.874 --> 00:05:55.680
was that the only reason Google was allowed&nbsp;
in China was because China needed Google. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.680 --> 00:06:02.220
the CCP bit their tongue and let Google carry on&nbsp;
with their shenanigans. But, in the end, it seemed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.220 --> 00:06:06.720
like Google was winning. They were successfully&nbsp;
fighting Chinese censorship by expanding to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.720 --> 00:06:13.500
China. Not to mention, they were making a bunch&nbsp;
of money. By late 2009, Google controlled over a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.500 --> 00:06:19.380
third of the Chinese search market. But then,&nbsp;
if things were going so well, what happened?

00:06:19.380 --> 00:06:28.140
THE CCP BACKSTABS:&nbsp;

00:06:28.140 --> 00:06:33.060
Ironically, it wasn’t actually censorship&nbsp;
concerns that pushed Google out of China.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.060 --> 00:06:38.040
For the most part, China was letting Google have&nbsp;
their way when it came to pushing the boundaries.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.040 --> 00:06:43.920
The factor that actually pushed out Google was&nbsp;
Chinese greed. Remember how we talked about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.920 --> 00:06:49.740
the CCP wanting Google to help develop China’s&nbsp;
tech industry. Well, that was going pretty well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.740 --> 00:06:55.620
but China wanted more progress. China didn’t want&nbsp;
to wait out the full cycle of engineers working at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.620 --> 00:07:00.420
Google, then leaving Google and starting their own&nbsp;
companies, and then hiring more Chinese engineers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.420 --> 00:07:05.340
and training them, and so on. It would take&nbsp;
several years if not decades for this to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.340 --> 00:07:10.020
naturally play out and for Google’s knowledge&nbsp;
to spread across the Chinese tech industry. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.020 --> 00:07:15.600
China figured, why wait that long when&nbsp;
we can just have it now and with that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.600 --> 00:07:22.500
China would launch operation Aurora. Starting in&nbsp;
mid 2009, China would start a massive cyber attack&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.500 --> 00:07:28.080
not just against Google but against most of the&nbsp;
American tech industry. This included companies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.080 --> 00:07:34.260
like Yahoo, Adobe, Juniper Networks, and even&nbsp;
Morgan Stanley. Their target at each of these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.260 --> 00:07:39.660
firms was their source code which was ironically&nbsp;
something that these firms didn’t really protect.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.660 --> 00:07:44.820
They were spending billions on protecting personal&nbsp;
data, financial profiles, and credit card numbers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.820 --> 00:07:49.620
but they had never considered protecting their&nbsp;
source code to the same caliber. Their secret&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.620 --> 00:07:55.500
sauce was really their branding, positioning,&nbsp;
and user base, not their source code. I mean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.500 --> 00:08:00.360
just because you or me have Google’s source&nbsp;
code doesn't mean that we’ll become the next&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.360 --> 00:08:06.900
Google. For China, however, that was actually the&nbsp;
case. With the source code of these companies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.900 --> 00:08:13.200
the CCP could create their own tech giants that&nbsp;
their citizens would have to use. This of course&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.200 --> 00:08:19.380
put off Google but it didn’t push them over&nbsp;
the edge just yet. The reality was that many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.380 --> 00:08:25.770
of their biggest projects like Android and Chrome&nbsp;
were already open source, so it’s not like China&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.770 --> 00:08:32.160
stole too much, but they were by no means done.&nbsp;
Next, the CCP would target Google’s intellectual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.160 --> 00:08:38.220
property which was much more valuable. But even&nbsp;
this, didn’t push Google over the edge. The nail&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.220 --> 00:08:44.340
in the coffin came soon after though when the CCP&nbsp;
started hacking into Gmail. But, wait a minute,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.340 --> 00:08:50.100
there’s nothing special about Gmail. If Google was&nbsp;
willing to overlook intellectual property theft,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.100 --> 00:08:55.440
why did they care about Gmail so much?&nbsp;
Well, it wasn’t for the source code. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.440 --> 00:09:00.300
CCP couldn’t care less about the source code of&nbsp;
an email platform. What they were really after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.300 --> 00:09:06.240
was human rights activists. They were looking to&nbsp;
identify the individuals behind problematic email&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.240 --> 00:09:14.040
addresses so that they could go ahead and off&nbsp;
them. Google had had it with the CCP and their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.040 --> 00:09:20.520
antics. On January 12, 2010, they would publicly&nbsp;
state “We have decided we are no longer willing to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.520 --> 00:09:26.220
continue censoring our results on Google.cn, and&nbsp;
so over the next few weeks we will be discussing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.220 --> 00:09:30.660
with the Chinese government the basis on which&nbsp;
we could operate an unfiltered search engine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.660 --> 00:09:36.300
within the law, if at all.” And this leads us&nbsp;
to the dramatic withdrawal that we detailed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.300 --> 00:09:42.000
at the beginning of the video. But, as we also&nbsp;
mentioned, this withdrawal didn’t last forever.

00:09:42.000 --> 00:09:44.340
GOOGLE CRAWLS BACK:&nbsp;

00:09:44.340 --> 00:09:54.600
The bottomline was that Google didn’t realize how&nbsp;
independent China’s tech industry had become since&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.600 --> 00:10:00.060
their entry. And instead of getting crushed by&nbsp;
censorship, the Chinese tech industry actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.060 --> 00:10:06.180
blew up in the early 2010s without Google.&nbsp;
Companies like Xiaomi, Tencent, Alibaba,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.180 --> 00:10:12.060
Huawei, Baidu, and JD.com were crushing it&nbsp;
growing to multi deca billion if not multi&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.060 --> 00:10:17.460
hundred billion dollar valuations. And unlike&nbsp;
Google, these companies were willing to play by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.460 --> 00:10:24.240
any rules the CCP set. You could say that Google&nbsp;
had a bit of FOMO, but under Larry’s leadership,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.240 --> 00:10:28.560
Google wasn’t wiling to backtrack on their&nbsp;
decision to leave China. All of this would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.560 --> 00:10:34.140
change when Sundar Pichai took over though. It’s&nbsp;
not that Sundar is evil or that he loves the CCP,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.140 --> 00:10:40.620
but as a non founder CEO, he did have the pressure&nbsp;
to constantly please shareholders. He couldn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:40.620 --> 00:10:46.080
just ignore them like most founders tend to do.&nbsp;
And from his perspective, the easiest way to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.080 --> 00:10:51.660
continue growth was attacking the Chinese market.&nbsp;
This is exactly what Apple was doing and they were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.660 --> 00:10:57.600
getting extraordinary results. So, Sundar started&nbsp;
off by appealing to Chinese developers. He would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:57.600 --> 00:11:02.700
host a developer conference in China at the&nbsp;
end of 2016 where a bunch of Chinese specific&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.700 --> 00:11:09.120
development tools would be announced. But, he&nbsp;
wasn’t happy with just this. What he really wanted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.120 --> 00:11:15.180
was to bring back Google search to China, but&nbsp;
given their dramatic exit, China wasn’t exactly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.180 --> 00:11:21.960
ecstatic to let them back in. Not to mention, they&nbsp;
no longer needed Google either. So, Google had to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:21.960 --> 00:11:28.140
convince China to let them back in which brings&nbsp;
us to project Dragonfly. On the surface, Dragonfly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.140 --> 00:11:33.900
was just a censored version of Google search, but&nbsp;
behind the scenes, it was a lot more nefarious.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:33.900 --> 00:11:38.460
According to the Incercept, the project allegedly&nbsp;
entailed collecting peoples search history,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:38.460 --> 00:11:43.200
location history, and other private data and&nbsp;
sharing all of it with a Chinese company,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:43.200 --> 00:11:48.840
presumably for surveillance purposes. When this&nbsp;
got leaked to the media though, Google not only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:48.840 --> 00:11:54.180
had to deal with a bunch of public backlash&nbsp;
but also a bunch of internal protests. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:54.180 --> 00:11:59.220
unsurprisingly, they would kill the project&nbsp;
just a few months later at the end of 2018.

00:11:59.220 --> 00:12:01.080
GOOGLE’S FUTURE IN CHINA:&nbsp;

00:12:01.080 --> 00:12:10.740
Since all of this took place, privacy&nbsp;
has become a top concern when it comes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:10.740 --> 00:12:15.180
to these big tech companies, so Sundar has very&nbsp;
much shifted his tone when it comes to China.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:15.180 --> 00:12:21.120
He has since clarified that the company has no&nbsp;
plans of relaunching in China. Who knows though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:21.120 --> 00:12:25.560
with the current recession and stagnating&nbsp;
growth, China may very well be something that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:25.560 --> 00:12:30.840
Google considers once again. But, the truth&nbsp;
is that whether they reenter China or not,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:30.840 --> 00:12:38.220
they have already lost to the CCP. The reality is&nbsp;
that the CCP played Google brilliantly. They lured&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:38.220 --> 00:12:42.540
them in with the appeal of a large consumer&nbsp;
market. Then, they stole all of their source&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:42.540 --> 00:12:48.960
code and IP. And finally, they pushed them out&nbsp;
never to return. Meanwhile, Google was led to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:48.960 --> 00:12:54.180
believe that they were doing the moral thing by&nbsp;
stepping outside of China when in reality, they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:54.180 --> 00:13:00.060
were just making room for up and coming Chinese&nbsp;
companies to take their place. And today, it’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:00.060 --> 00:13:07.140
Google that wants China, not the other way around&nbsp;
and that’s the sad story of Google China. Do you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:07.140 --> 00:13:12.360
think Google should have left China? Comment that&nbsp;
